{
 "id": "2026-zumrut-color",
 "name": "Color Contrast Arrangements Harmony, Preference and Liking",
 "description": "Faithful replication with the original images: twelve colored versions of one simplified street silhouette of five buildings inspired by traditional Istanbul houses, drawn by the authors on a mid-grey background, each colored with one Color Contrast Arrangement of three Munsell colors: single contrasts (hue, value, chroma; each with warm and cool colors) and double contrasts (equivalent hues, values or chromas with a dominant contrast), two examples of each, with the largest facade as the emphasized element. As in the paper's first survey (Q1), participants first answer background questions (gender, age, education, profession, color vision deficiency, education in color-related subjects, professional involvement in color), then see the twelve visuals one after another and for each rate the paper's three statements, The colors in the visual are harmonious with one another, I would prefer to see this color arrangement in my surroundings and I liked the color arrangement in the visual, on its 3-point scale (1 = negative, 3 = positive), and give three adjectives that best describe the visual. Rebuilt from Zümrüt Tokçiftçi & Küçükkılıç Özcan (2026), Evaluation of Color Contrast Arrangements in Terms of Harmony, Preference and Liking, Buildings 16(14): 2906, with the visuals cropped from its Table 3 (the visual numbers trimmed off) and the statements, scale and background topics from its Section 3 (CC BY 4.0). Image ids: CA01-CA12 (the paper's arrangement numbers); answers are in harmony, preference, liking and adjectives, keyed by image id; the arrangement type of each is stored in the page's original-study block. Analysis as in the paper: exclude participants reporting a color vision deficiency, then for each arrangement sum the shares of positive (3) and moderate (2) answers per statement and rank the twelve arrangements on harmony, preference and liking, compare the rankings with Spearman correlations, and count the adjectives; the paper (109 participants with normal color vision, Google Forms) found value-contrast arrangements most harmonious and equivalent chroma and hue arrangements least, CA08 (equivalent hues, value contrast dominant) first in harmony and liking, CA12 last in all three, and cool schemes generally preferred to warm ones. Differences from the original study: (1) only the first survey is included; the second survey's nine bipolar adjective pairs are not, because the paper prints only their positive ends (organized, balanced, similar, good, peaceful, relaxing, beautiful, tasteful, pleasant); (2) the background question wording, the middle label of the scale (neutral), the instruction text and the order of the visuals (CA01 to CA12, as numbered in the paper) are ours; (3) the visuals are the copies printed in Table 3 (about 420 px wide, shown enlarged), not the original files; (4) the paper recruited through social media, email and messaging applications; here anyone can take part.",
